// LoopInvariantCodeMotion — a Std->Std pass that moves a computation whose answer cannot change across
// a loop's iterations OUT of the loop, into the block that runs once before it. `for v in a` over an
// `Array with Integer` stops reloading the array's length and buffer base on every element.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS TWO PHASES WITH TWO DIFFERENT SAFETY ARGUMENTS, AND CONFLATING THEM WOULD BE THE BUG.**
// Over both of them sits ONE bound that is neither — **Rule 3**, which is about REGISTERS rather than
// about correctness, and which is the single largest restriction in the pass.
//
//   • **PURE COMPUTATION** (`docs/emitted-code-roadmap.md`, `EC14` phase 1). An arithmetic op the
//     dialect declares `isPure` may be "duplicated, reordered, or dropped" — those are `StdOpMeta`'s
//     own words — so moving one to a point that DOMINATES it needs no further argument. It cannot
//     fault (`div`/`mod` trap and are `isPure: false`, so the arithmetic roster answers `neither` for
//     them), it cannot be observed, and the preheader dominates every block of the loop, so every use
//     still follows its def. v1's LICM is exactly this phase, and its own comment states the same
//     reason: *"No dominance-of-exits check needed: we only hoist pure ops."*
//
//   • **INVARIANT LOADS** (phase 2), which is where the anchor loop's win is and which is `isPure:
//     false`. A load carries BOTH questions the pure case does not: may the location change under the
//     loop, and may moving the read make it happen when it would not have? The two rules below answer
//     them separately, and each is stated as a RULE rather than assumed.
//
// ══ RULE 1 — WHEN A LOAD IS INVARIANT: THE LOOP WRITES NOTHING ══
//
// **A load in a loop whose blocks contain no op that writes memory reads a location the loop cannot
// change.** "Writes memory" is asked of the dialect as `isStore or isCall` — which is every op except
// the arithmetic band, the pure ADDRESS ops, the branches, the RETURNS, `div`/`mod` and the two loads
// (the exact census is at `scanLoopEffects`, where it is measured rather than recalled) — so a loop
// holding a call, a store, an atomic, an OS primitive or a `memFill` is refused outright and no load in
// it moves.
//
// ⭐ This is DELIBERATELY not an alias analysis, and it is not an approximation of one — it is a
// different rule that happens to be sufficient for the shape this rung exists for. `EC15` made the
// anchor loop CALL-FREE (it removed the stride fork and the `__managed_get_unchecked` it protected), so
// the rule wins that loop outright. A loop that stores is left alone entirely rather than reasoned
// about, and `loadIndirect`'s own variant comment names the program that would break under a weaker
// rule: `specs/short-circuit-evaluation.md`'s `short-circuit-in-while-condition` reads AND WRITES one
// global every iteration, so its store is what refuses it.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT THE RULE DOES NOT COVER, STATED:** another OS THREAD writing the location while this loop
// spins. A spin-wait with no call in it would be hoisted into an infinite loop. Nothing in this
// compiler's own sources or the stdlib spells one — the green-thread substrate yields through a CALL
// (`Runtime.yield`), and every real-thread handshake in the runtime band goes through `osWaitHandle` or
// an atomic — and the runtime band is not run through this pipeline at all (see `hoistLoopInvariants`).
// This is the one premise here that is about the whole program rather than about the loop.
//
// ⚠ The frontend proves something adjacent and it is NOT what this rule rests on. A `for … in`
// element is a LEXICAL BORROW of the subject, and the borrow checker refuses a call taking that subject
// mutably while the loop is live (`Parser.maxon`, the ⚖ 2026-08-26 `EC3` ruling; round-trip cases in
// `specs-shv2/borrow-liveness.md`). That is a real fact and it is cited here so a later reader does not
// think it was missed — but it says nothing about a `while` loop, about a global, or about a container
// the loop does not iterate, and Rule 1 needs none of it.
//
// ══ RULE 2 — WHEN A LOAD MAY BE SPECULATED: IT WAS GOING TO RUN ANYWAY ══
//
// **A hoisted op executes even when the loop body never runs**, and for a load that can FAULT. The
// anchor makes the distinction concrete: the array LENGTH is loaded in the loop HEADER, which runs the
// moment the preheader does, so hoisting it is free; the BUFFER BASE is loaded in the block the header's
// guard branches to, which for an EMPTY array never runs at all.
//
// Two admissions, and a load needs one of them:
//
//   **2a — MUST-EXECUTE.** The load's block dominates every block through which control LEAVES the loop
//   AND every LATCH. Any execution that either iterates or exits has therefore already run the block.
//   `markMustExecuteBlocks` computes it. ⚠ The one residual, stated: an execution that enters the loop and
//   NEITHER iterates nor exits — it diverges inside the first iteration — has not run the block, so a
//   program that would have HUNG can instead FAULT. With Rule 1 in force the loop holds no call, so
//   that requires an inner infinite loop; this is the standard imprecision of speculative LICM and it
//   is accepted rather than unnoticed.
//
//   **2b — A DEREFERENCEABILITY WITNESS.** The same loop is already hoisting, under 2a, another load
//   through the SAME address value whose `coverage` (offset + width) is at least this one's. That load
//   runs unconditionally once hoisted, so the object at that address spans at least `coverage` bytes —
//   and a `loadIndirect`'s `(addrId, offset)` pair names field `offset` OF THAT OBJECT, with `ByteOffset`
//   non-negative by type. A field inside a span the program is already proving readable is readable.
//   ⚠ **THE PREMISE, STATED: an address value is a base plus a NON-NEGATIVE displacement into one
//   object.** That is how every address in this tier is formed (`globalAddr`, `stackRecordAddr`, an
//   allocation's result, `base + index*stride`), and it is what makes "covers more" imply "covers less".
//   ⚠ **HALF OF IT IS ENFORCED AND HALF IS STRUCTURAL, and which is which matters.** ENFORCED: the
//   displacement cannot be negative (`ByteOffset` is `int(0 to u64.max)`, so the Std tier cannot spell
//   `[addr - k]`), and both loads must name the SAME `ValueId`, which the witness table keys on — a
//   differently-biased base is a different value and finds no witness. STRUCTURAL, and checked by nobody:
//   that the object at that `ValueId` begins at or before it. Unlike `requireArithOpIsPure` beside it,
//   there is no flag to re-ask; a guard would have to enumerate the address-producing shapes, which is
//   the enumerated-roster failure this file otherwise avoids.
//   Only the SMALLER-or-equal extent is ever admitted, which is the safe direction: this rule can shrink
//   a proven span, never extend one.
//   ⭐ 2b is what takes the anchor loop from 7 instructions to 6. The length load at `[a+8]` covers 16
//   bytes under 2a; the buffer load at `[a+0]` needs 8, and `a` is the same value.
//
// ══ RULE 3 — A LOOP HOLDING A CALL HOISTS NOTHING, AND THIS ONE IS ABOUT REGISTERS ══
//
// Rules 1 and 2 are correctness. **Rule 3 is a BOUND**, it applies to BOTH phases — arithmetic as well
// as loads — and it is the largest thing this pass declines to do. A hoisted value is live across the
// WHOLE loop by construction, and a live range crossing a CALL is confined to the five callee-saved
// registers x64-windows leaves. `EC13` measured one end of that: shv2 REFUSES rather than spills, and a
// SINGLE reused expression across a call took `witness-dispatch-inside-a-pressured-loop` red with
// `E5001`. This rung measured the other end — what happens when the allocator does NOT refuse: with the
// bound off, 48 `map` fragments gained **+96 `loadRegSlot` and +48 `storeSlotReg`** and every one of
// their frames grew, because the hoisted value was COLD-SPILLED and reloaded. In `Map.grow` the whole
// trade was one `leaRegRegImm32` replaced by one `loadRegSlot`: an ALU op for a memory op, which is not
// a win at any instruction count.
//
// ⇒ It is a structural rule and not a budget with a number in it. And it costs the anchor NOTHING,
// which is what makes it affordable: `EC15` made that loop call-free, and `regalloc/many-call-crossing`
// — where nine invariant computations DO leave a loop — holds no call either. Both were checked before
// the rule was adopted, and the second is the committed fragment that would move if it were dropped.
//
// ⚠ **IT MAKES RULE 1's `isCall` TERM DEAD AT ITS ONLY READER**, and that is deliberate rather than
// overlooked: `hoistIntoPreheader` returns on Rule 3 before Rule 1 is ever asked, so by then
// `writesMemory` is exactly "some op is `isStore`". Rule 1 keeps its own `isCall` term because it is a
// statement about MEMORY that must stay true if Rule 3 is ever relaxed — the two are independent
// questions that happen to share a walk.
//
// ⚠ **IT SEES MEMBER BLOCKS ONLY.** A call on a loop-EXIT path is not counted, so a hoisted value read
// after the loop can still have a range crossing one. That is a gap in the heuristic and not in the
// safety argument; a stricter version needs a per-block call summary this rung does not have, exactly as
// `EC13`'s own barrier note records.
//
// ⚠ **NO SPEC CASE GOES RED IF RULE 3 IS DELETED**, and the spec says so. Its only pin is the minted
// `map` and `url` fragments — a fragment pin, which by this project's own standard is the weaker kind.
// A pressure heuristic has no wrong answer to catch it with; that is stated rather than papered over.
//
// ══ WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HOISTED ══
//
// - **`const`.** `classifyArithOperands` answers `neither` for it, so it never reaches the admission at
//   all — and that is the right answer for the same reason `EC13` gives: rematerializing a constant is
//   cheaper than a live range that spans the loop, and v1's own Stage A header records that a hoisted
//   canonical constant "conflicts with ABI-constrained uses". A constant hoist is its own rung.
// - **The three pure ADDRESS ops** (`globalAddr`, `rdataAddr`, `funcAddr`) and `stackRecordAddr`. They
//   are `isPure: true` and ALWAYS invariant (they read no value at all), so they need no analysis — and
//   hoisting them is therefore a pure register-pressure trade with no instruction-count argument behind
//   it. `EC13` left the same three open for the same reason; this rung does not take them either.
// - **`div`/`mod`.** They TRAP, the dialect says so with `isPure: false`, and the flag is honoured
//   rather than reasoned around — `EC12` and `EC13` both declined them and this makes three.
// - **A loop with no preheader**, and **a loop whose preheader can reach the header without entering
//   it**. See `soleLoopEntry`.
//
// ══ MECHANISM REUSED RATHER THAN BUILT ══
//
// `IR/NaturalLoops.maxon` finds the back edges and the member sets — the SAME finder the Target tier's
// loop depth and `E5001` region use, moved there by this rung rather than written a second time.
// `StdDominatorTree` answers dominance and hands back the CFG adjacency it already built.
// `classifyArithOperands` / `classifyLoadOperands` / `stdOpDefinedValue` are the dialect's three
// answers to what an op computes, reads and defines. Nothing here matches `StdOp` itself.

// Hoist every loop-invariant computation throughout `module`, in place.
//
// Where the pass is scheduled and why: `PassPipeline.buildLoweringPasses`, between
// `commonSubexpressionElimination` and `promoteStackRecords`.
//
// ⭐ **AFTER CSE, NOT BEFORE, and v1 orders its own pipeline the same way** (`canonicalize -> cse ->
// licm -> dce`). Two reasons, and both are about this compiler rather than about the textbook: CSE has
// already collapsed a repeated invariant expression to ONE op, so this pass moves one instruction
// instead of three and lengthens one live range instead of three; and CSE's inputs are canonicalized by
// `foldConstOperands`, which is what turns `x * 31` into a single `binOpImm` whose literal is not a
// value at all — an invariant expression whose constant were still a separate `const` op would be
// refused here, because a `const` is never hoisted and would read as a variant operand.
//
// It runs before `promoteStackRecords` because that pass is deliberately last: its header says it
// decides the FINAL shape of the code, and its escape scan should see the addresses where they will
// actually be computed.
//
// Like `foldConstants` and CSE it takes no start index: its other caller would be the appended runtime
// band, and those hand-built bodies (`MmRuntime`, `GtRuntime`, the synthesized cascades) are written as
// straight-line Std IR with their loops already hand-hoisted — a second run there would walk every
// appended function to find nothing, and it is also what keeps Rule 1's thread premise narrow, because
// the runtime's own handshakes never pass through here.
export function hoistLoopInvariants(module StdModule)
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "hoistLoopInvariants: {module.funcCount()} function(s)")

	// ONE set of scratch for the whole module. Every column in it is cleared per loop and reused at the
	// size the largest loop so far needed, rather than reallocated — the shape `CseScratch` states and
	// `foldConstants.evaluateOps` gives the reason for.
	var scratch = LicmScratch.create()

	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `module`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for fnIndex in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		hoistInFunc(module, func: try module.functions.get(fnIndex) otherwise panic("hoistLoopInvariants: fnIndex {fnIndex} is below module.functions.count()"), scratch: scratch)
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'hoistLoopInvariants'

// Hoist out of every natural loop of one function.
//
// ⭐ **INNERMOST LOOP FIRST, AND EACH LOOP'S HOISTS ARE APPLIED BEFORE THE NEXT IS ANALYSED**, which is
// what lets a value escape a whole NEST in one run of the pass. An op moved to an inner loop's preheader
// is still inside the outer loop, so the outer loop's analysis sees it in a member block and can move it
// again. `distinctLoopHeaders` returns headers in back-edge discovery order, which is innermost-first
// for a nest (its header says why); a different order would hoist less, never wrongly.
//
// ⚠ Applying a hoist moves op REFERENCES between blocks and creates and deletes no block, so the
// dominator tree, the CFG adjacency, the back edges and every member set stay valid across it. That is
// why they are built ONCE per function here rather than per loop.
function hoistInFunc(module StdModule, func IrFunction, scratch LicmScratch)
	let blockCount = func.blockRefs.count()

	// Fewer than two blocks cannot hold a loop this pass can serve: the only loop a one-block function
	// can have is a self-edge on the ENTRY, and the entry has no predecessor outside the loop to hoist
	// into. Declarations (no blocks at all) fall out here too.
	if blockCount < 2 'noLoopWithAPreheader'
		return
	end 'noLoopWithAPreheader'

	// ⭐ **THE BACK-EDGE QUESTION COMES FIRST, AND IT NEEDS ONLY THE SUCCESSORS.** NO BACK EDGE, NO LOOP —
	// and that is MOST functions, which is why the dominator tree is built AFTER this test rather than
	// before it: a loopless function would otherwise pay for a predecessor graph, a reverse postorder, the
	// dominator solve and a child adjacency, all to be told there was no loop to use them on.
	let succ = buildStdSuccessorGraph(module, func: func, blockCount: blockCount)

	var backEdges = LoopBackEdges.create()
	findLoopBackEdges(succ, nodeCount: blockCount, edges: backEdges)

	if backEdges.count() == 0 'loopless'
		return
	end 'loopless'

	let tree = StdDominatorTree.buildFromSuccessors(succ, blockCount: blockCount)

	for header in distinctLoopHeaders(backEdges, nodeCount: blockCount) 'eachLoop'
		hoistFromLoop(module, func: func, tree: tree, backEdges: backEdges, header: header as BlockRefIndex, scratch: scratch)
	end 'eachLoop'
end 'hoistInFunc'

// Analyse ONE natural loop and move what it admits into that loop's preheader.
function hoistFromLoop(module StdModule, func IrFunction, tree StdDominatorTree, backEdges LoopBackEdges, header BlockRefIndex, scratch LicmScratch)
	scratch.beginLoop(tree.blockCount)
	collectNaturalLoop(tree.pred, header: header as CsrIndex, edges: backEdges, nodeCount: tree.blockCount as CsrIndex, into: scratch.members)

	match soleLoopEntry(tree, members: scratch.members, header: header) 'preheader'
		none then return
		position(preheader) then hoistIntoPreheader(module, func: func, tree: tree, backEdges: backEdges, header: header, preheader: preheader, scratch: scratch)
	end 'preheader'
end 'hoistFromLoop'

// The half of `hoistFromLoop` that runs once a preheader is known — split out so the `match` above has
// nothing but the two answers in it.
function hoistIntoPreheader(module StdModule, func IrFunction, tree StdDominatorTree, backEdges LoopBackEdges, header BlockRefIndex, preheader BlockRefIndex, scratch LicmScratch)
	scanLoopEffects(module, func: func, scratch: scratch)
	if not scratch.effects.admitsAnyHoist() 'aCallCrossesEveryHoistedRange'
		return
	end 'aCallCrossesEveryHoistedRange'

	markMustExecuteBlocks(tree, backEdges: backEdges, header: header, scratch: scratch)
	collectLoopDefs(module, func: func, scratch: scratch)
	analyzeLoopBlocks(module, func: func, tree: tree, header: header, memoryIsStable: scratch.effects.memoryIsStable(), scratch: scratch)

	if scratch.hoisted.count() == 0 'nothingInvariant'
		return
	end 'nothingInvariant'

	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "hoistLoopInvariants: '{String.from(func.name)}' loop at block position {header} — {scratch.hoisted.count()} op(s) to preheader {preheader}")
	appendToPreheader(module, func: func, preheader: preheader, ops: scratch.hoisted)
end 'hoistIntoPreheader'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The preheader
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// How many predecessors of a loop header lie outside the loop — the number `soleLoopEntry` needs to be
// exactly one. A COUNT, not a block position, which is why it is not a `BlockRefIndex`.
typealias PredecessorCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// Which block a loop is entered THROUGH, when there is exactly one and it goes nowhere else.
union LoopPreheader
	none
	position(preheader BlockRefIndex)
end 'LoopPreheader'

// The loop's preheader: the unique predecessor of `header` outside the loop, whose ONLY successor is
// the header.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SINGLE-SUCCESSOR CONDITION IS NOT TIDINESS — IT IS HALF OF RULE 2.** An op placed in the
// preheader runs when the preheader runs. If the preheader's branch could go somewhere OTHER than the
// header, that is a path on which the loop is never entered and the hoisted op runs anyway — which for
// a pure op is merely wasteful but for a LOAD is speculation with no argument behind it at all, because
// the "it was going to execute" reasoning of 2a is about executions that ENTER the loop. The parser
// emits exactly such a preheader for `for v in <an empty Iterable>` (`EmptyIterableExit`), so this is a
// live shape and not a hypothetical one.
//
// ⚠ **NO PREHEADER IS CREATED WHEN THERE IS NONE.** Splitting an edge to manufacture one means minting a
// block and re-pointing branch-edge argument lists — a CFG change this rung does not make, and one whose
// interaction with `branchEdges` (the phi model the register allocator still consumes) is a rung of its
// own. A loop entered from two places, or through a conditional branch, is skipped whole.
function soleLoopEntry(tree StdDominatorTree, members LoopMembership, header BlockRefIndex) returns LoopPreheader
	var found = LoopPreheader.none
	var entries = 0 as PredecessorCount

	for k in 0 upto tree.pred.degree(header as CsrIndex) 'eachPredecessor'
		let predecessor = tree.pred.neighbour(header as CsrIndex, k: k)
		if members.contains(predecessor) 'aLatch'
			continue
		end 'aLatch'

		entries = entries + 1
		found = LoopPreheader.position(predecessor as BlockRefIndex)
	end 'eachPredecessor'

	if entries != 1 'notAUniqueEntry'
		return LoopPreheader.none
	end 'notAUniqueEntry'

	return soleEntryLeadsOnlyToTheHeader(tree, entry: found, header: header)
end 'soleLoopEntry'

// Whether the ONE entry predecessor found above goes nowhere but the loop header — the second half of
// `soleLoopEntry`'s condition, applied to the candidate the first half produced.
function soleEntryLeadsOnlyToTheHeader(tree StdDominatorTree, entry LoopPreheader, header BlockRefIndex) returns LoopPreheader
	match entry 'candidate'
		none then panic("soleEntryLeadsOnlyToTheHeader: asked of no candidate — the caller counted exactly one entry predecessor before calling")
		position(preheader) then return entry if tree.succ.degree(preheader as CsrIndex) == 1 and tree.succ.neighbour(preheader as CsrIndex, k: 0) as BlockRefIndex == header else LoopPreheader.none
	end 'candidate'
end 'soleEntryLeadsOnlyToTheHeader'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Rule 2a — the blocks an execution of the loop must have run
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Mark, in `scratch`, the blocks that dominate EVERY block through which control leaves the loop AND
// every LATCH — i.e. the blocks an execution that either iterates or exits has certainly run.
//
// ⭐ **ONE CLIMB PER NAMED BLOCK, NOT A DOMINANCE QUERY PER PAIR.** Each exit and latch walks its own
// immediate-dominator chain up to the header, incrementing a counter on every block it passes; a block
// whose counter equals the number of climbs is on all of them, which is exactly "dominates all of them".
// That is O(named × depth) where asking a `dominates(a, b)` predicate of every loop block against every
// named block would have been O(members × named × depth). The header is on every chain by construction
// (it dominates every member), so it always qualifies — which is the common case and the anchor's.
//
// ⚠ A duplicate in the named set (a latch that is also an exit) is harmless: its chain is climbed twice
// and the required count is two higher, so the equality still selects exactly the blocks on all chains.
function markMustExecuteBlocks(tree StdDominatorTree, backEdges LoopBackEdges, header BlockRefIndex, scratch LicmScratch)
	for position in scratch.members.blocks 'eachExit'
		if not blockLeavesTheLoop(tree, position: position as BlockRefIndex, members: scratch.members) 'notAnExit'
			continue
		end 'notAnExit'

		scratch.mustExecute.climbChainFrom(position as BlockRefIndex, header: header, tree: tree)
	end 'eachExit'

	for k in 0 upto backEdges.count() 'eachLatch'
		let h = (try backEdges.heads.get(k) otherwise panic("markMustExecuteBlocks: heads.get OOB at {k}")) as BlockRefIndex
		if h != header 'anotherLoop'
			continue
		end 'anotherLoop'

		scratch.mustExecute.climbChainFrom((try backEdges.tails.get(k) otherwise panic("markMustExecuteBlocks: tails.get OOB at {k}") as BlockRefIndex), header: header, tree: tree)
	end 'eachLatch'
end 'markMustExecuteBlocks'

// Whether control can leave the loop from `position` — it has a successor outside the member set.
function blockLeavesTheLoop(tree StdDominatorTree, position BlockRefIndex, members LoopMembership) returns bool
	for k in 0 upto tree.succ.degree(position as CsrIndex) 'eachSuccessor'
		if not members.contains(tree.succ.neighbour(position as CsrIndex, k: k)) 'anExitEdge'
			return true
		end 'anExitEdge'
	end 'eachSuccessor'

	return false
end 'blockLeavesTheLoop'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Rule 1 — does the loop write memory at all
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// What one loop's ops say about the two questions this pass asks of them, filled by ONE walk because
// they are answered from the same two metadata flags and a second walk would read the same ops twice.
type LoopEffects
	// Any op declaring `isStore` or `isCall` — i.e. anything that can change a location. Rule 1.
	var writesMemory as bool
	// Any op declaring `isCall`. Rule 3.
	var holdsACall as bool

	static function create() returns LoopEffects
		return Self{writesMemory: false, holdsACall: false}
	end 'create'

	function reset()
		self.writesMemory = false
		self.holdsACall = false
	end 'reset'

	// Rule 1: a loop that changes no location cannot change what a load in it reads.
	function memoryIsStable() returns bool
		return not self.writesMemory
	end 'memoryIsStable'

	// Rule 3: a loop holding a call hoists NOTHING, loads or arithmetic. See `scanLoopEffects`.
	function admitsAnyHoist() returns bool
		return not self.holdsACall
	end 'admitsAnyHoist'

	function note(op StdOp)
		self.writesMemory = self.writesMemory or op.rawValue.isStore or op.rawValue.isCall
		self.holdsACall = self.holdsACall or op.rawValue.isCall
	end 'note'
end 'LoopEffects'

// Fill `scratch.effects` from every op of the loop, asking the DIALECT's own metadata rather than a list
// of op names inside this pass.
//
// ⭐ **RULE 1 — `isStore or isCall` IS EXACTLY "CAN CHANGE A LOCATION".** MEASURED against the union
// 2026-08-28: **20 of the 99 variants declare neither flag**, and every one of them is either pure
// arithmetic (`const`, `binOp`, `unaryOp`, `cmp`, the two `Imm` forms), a pure ADDRESS (`globalAddr`,
// `rdataAddr`, `funcAddr`, `stackRecordAddr`), a branch (`branch`, `condBranch`, `switch`), a RETURN
// (`ret`, `retVoid`, `errorReturn`), a trapping division (`div`, `mod`) or a READ (`loadIndirect`,
// `tlsSlotLoad`). None writes anything, so the test admits precisely the loops in which no location can
// change.
//
// ⛔ **AN EARLIER SPELLING OF THIS CENSUS NAMED SEVEN AND WAS WRONG** — it listed the IMPURE seven and
// called them "the only variants", which omitted the thirteen pure ones and, worse, told a reader that a
// loop containing a `return` was refused here. It is not, and the reason it is still safe is the
// paragraph below. The same wrong census had a second copy in `docs/emitted-code-roadmap.md`; both were
// corrected together, because one fact written down twice is this project's signature bug.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A `return` INSIDE THE LOOP IS SAFE, AND NOT BECAUSE THIS SCAN REFUSES IT.** A block ending in
// `ret`/`retVoid`/`errorReturn` has NO CFG successors, so it cannot be on a path to a latch, so
// `collectNaturalLoop` never admits it — it is a NON-MEMBER successor of the block that branches to it,
// which makes that predecessor an EXIT under `blockLeavesTheLoop` and forces `markMustExecuteBlocks` to
// climb from it. A block that does not run on the return path therefore cannot dominate all exits, and
// Rule 2a refuses it. The return path is covered by the speculation rule, not by the effects scan.
//
// ⭐⭐ **RULE 3 — A LOOP HOLDING A CALL HOISTS NOTHING AT ALL, AND THAT BOUND IS MEASURED RATHER THAN
// CAUTIOUS.** `EC13` established the mechanism: a live range crossing a call is confined to the five
// callee-saved registers x64-windows leaves, and shv2 REFUSES rather than spills — one reused expression
// took `witness-dispatch-inside-a-pressured-loop` red with `E5001`. LICM's ranges span the WHOLE loop,
// which is strictly worse. What this rung MEASURED is the other half, in the case where the allocator
// does not refuse: with the barrier off, 48 `map` fragments gained **+96 `loadRegSlot` and +48
// `storeSlotReg`** and every one of their frames grew, because the hoisted value was COLD-SPILLED and
// reloaded. In `Map.grow` that trade is a `leaRegRegImm32` replaced by a `loadRegSlot` — an ALU op for a
// memory op, which is not a win at any count. ⇒ the bound is not a budget with a number in it; it is the
// structural rule that the ranges which cost most are the ones crossing a call.
//
// ⚠ It costs the anchor NOTHING, which is what makes it affordable: `EC15` made that loop call-free, and
// `regalloc/many-call-crossing`'s own loop — where nine invariant computations DO leave — holds no call
// either. Both were checked before the rule was adopted.
//
// The TERMINATOR is scanned too, not only the body, and ⚠ **that arm is currently DEAD — kept anyway,
// with the reason written down rather than the arm deleted.** The only terminator declaring either flag
// is `osExit`, which has no successors and therefore can never be in a natural loop's member set. What
// the scan defends against is a FUTURE terminator that both has successors and writes memory; asking is
// one field read per block, and a scan that read only `opRefs` would be answering the question about
// part of the loop.
function scanLoopEffects(module StdModule, func IrFunction, scratch LicmScratch)
	scratch.effects.reset()

	for position in scratch.members.blocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAtPosition(module, func: func, position: position as BlockRefIndex)

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			scratch.effects.note(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("scanLoopEffects: op index {opIdx} from block.opRefs not in module.ops"))
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
			scratch.effects.note(try module.ops.get(block.terminatorIndex()) otherwise panic("scanLoopEffects: terminator index not in module.ops"))
		end 'hasTerm'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'scanLoopEffects'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Invariance
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Record every value the loop DEFINES — the ones an operand test has to ask about. A value defined
// outside is invariant by construction and needs no entry.
//
// ⚠ **A BLOCK ARG IS A DEF, AND SO IS THE SECOND HALF OF A TWO-DEF OP.** The header's phis are how the
// index and every carried `var` come round the back edge, so they are the definition of "changes across
// iterations"; and `tryCall`/`witnessTryCall` write a result AND an error flag, which v1's LICM missed —
// its own comment records that consumers of the flag then "appear to read a value defined outside the
// loop and get hoisted, breaking the loop's exit condition". `stdOpDefinedValue` reports both halves,
// so there is nothing here to forget.
function collectLoopDefs(module StdModule, func IrFunction, scratch LicmScratch)
	for position in scratch.members.blocks 'eachBlock'
		let block = blockAtPosition(module, func: func, position: position as BlockRefIndex)

		for blockArg in block.blockArgs 'eachPhi'
			scratch.definedInLoop.insert(blockArg.valueId)
		end 'eachPhi'

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			recordDefsOf(module, opIdx: opIdx, definedInLoop: scratch.definedInLoop)
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'hasTerm'
			recordDefsOf(module, opIdx: block.terminatorIndex(), definedInLoop: scratch.definedInLoop)
		end 'hasTerm'
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'collectLoopDefs'

function recordDefsOf(module StdModule, opIdx OpIndex, definedInLoop LoopValueStamps)
	match stdOpDefinedValue(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("recordDefsOf: op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")) 'defines'
		none then break 'defines'
		defines(definedId) then definedInLoop.insert(definedId)
		definesPair(first, second) then insertBothDefs(definedInLoop, first: first, second: second)
	end 'defines'
end 'recordDefsOf'

function insertBothDefs(definedInLoop LoopValueStamps, first ValueId, second ValueId)
	definedInLoop.insert(first)
	definedInLoop.insert(second)
end 'insertBothDefs'

// Whether one operand is loop-invariant: either the loop does not define it at all, or this pass has
// already decided to hoist the op that does.
function operandIsInvariant(valueId ValueId, scratch LicmScratch) returns bool
	return not scratch.definedInLoop.contains(valueId) or scratch.invariant.contains(valueId)
end 'operandIsInvariant'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The analysis walk
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Walk the loop's blocks in DOMINATOR-TREE PREORDER from the header, deciding op by op, and rebuild
// each block's `opRefs` without the ops it gives up.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ONE PASS, NOT A FIXPOINT, AND SSA IS WHY.** A value's def DOMINATES its uses, so the defining
// block is an ANCESTOR of the reading block in the dominator tree and a preorder therefore visits every
// operand's def before the op that reads it. The one exception is a value carried round the BACK EDGE,
// which arrives as a header block-arg and is recorded variant before the walk starts. v1's LICM runs a
// `while changed` fixpoint over the loop's blocks for this; it does not need to, and the difference is a
// re-walk of every loop per round.
//
// ⭐ **THE HEADER'S DOMINATOR SUBTREE IS EXACTLY THE LOOP, PLUS SUBTREES THAT CAN BE PRUNED WHOLE.**
// Every member is dominated by the header, so all of them are in it; and a member's immediate dominator
// is itself a member (it lies on every path from the header to that member, and such a path stays inside
// the loop), so no member hangs below a non-member. That is what makes the prune below sound and the
// walk O(the loop) instead of O(the function) — the reverse-postorder sweep it replaces read every block
// of the function once per loop, which is the Θ(loops × blocks) `LoopMembership`'s header measures.
function analyzeLoopBlocks(module StdModule, func IrFunction, tree StdDominatorTree, header BlockRefIndex, memoryIsStable bool, scratch LicmScratch)
	var stack = BlockPositionColumn.create()
	stack.push(header)

	while stack.count() > 0 'preorder'
		let position = try stack.pop() otherwise panic("analyzeLoopBlocks: stack.pop on a non-empty stack failed")

		if not scratch.members.contains(position as CsrIndex) 'notInThisLoop'
			continue
		end 'notInThisLoop'

		analyzeLoopBlock(module, block: blockAtPosition(module, func: func, position: position), position: position, memoryIsStable: memoryIsStable, scratch: scratch)

		for k in 0 upto tree.children.degree(position as CsrIndex) 'eachChild'
			stack.push(tree.children.neighbour(position as CsrIndex, k: k))
		end 'eachChild'
	end 'preorder'
end 'analyzeLoopBlocks'

// Classify every body op of one loop block, moving the admitted ones onto the hoist list and keeping the
// rest.
//
// The block's column is rebuilt ONLY when something left it, for `removeOpsDefining`'s measured reason:
// a fresh `OpIndexArray` per block per loop is one allocation each for a column that mostly comes out
// identical. The terminator is not considered at all — it computes no value (`classifyArithOperands`
// answers `neither` for every control op) and moving one would leave the block with no exit.
function analyzeLoopBlock(module StdModule, block IrBlock, position BlockRefIndex, memoryIsStable bool, scratch LicmScratch)
	let mustExecute = scratch.mustExecute.holds(position)
	scratch.leaving.clear()

	for slot in 0 upto block.opRefs.count() 'eachOp'
		let opIdx = try block.opRefs.get(slot) otherwise panic("analyzeLoopBlock: opRefs.get OOB at {slot}")
		if not admitsOp(module, opIdx: opIdx, mustExecute: mustExecute, memoryIsStable: memoryIsStable, scratch: scratch) 'staysInTheLoop'
			continue
		end 'staysInTheLoop'

		scratch.leaving.push(slot)
		scratch.hoisted.push(opIdx)
	end 'eachOp'

	if scratch.leaving.count() == 0 'nothingLeft'
		return
	end 'nothingLeft'

	block.opRefs = keepingAllBut(block.opRefs, leaving: scratch.leaving)
end 'analyzeLoopBlock'

// `refs` without the entries at the ASCENDING positions in `leaving` — one pass, using the fact that the
// classification walk records positions in order.
//
// ⭐ A BLOCK THAT LOSES NOTHING KEEPS THE COLUMN IT ALREADY HAS, and its caller returns before reaching
// here. `removeOpsDefining` states the measurement behind that rule: an unconditional rebuild is one
// allocation per block per pass for a column that mostly comes out identical, and here it would be one
// per block per LOOP.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `removeOpsDefining` WITH A DIFFERENT ARGUMENT, and the difference is asymptotic rather
// than stylistic.** That function keys on a doomed-VALUE predicate and walks every block of the whole
// FUNCTION to find them, which is right for a pass that removes once per function and is exactly the
// Θ(loops × blocks) this pass spent a measurement removing. This keys on POSITIONS the classification
// walk has already recorded in order, so it touches only the one block it was handed.
//
// ⚠ One asymmetry with that function, stated because it is safe only for a reason: `removeOpsDefining`
// guards the TWO-DEF ops (`requireNeitherHalfIsDoomed` — removing one would delete the other's def), and
// this does not. It does not need to, because `tryCall`/`witnessTryCall` are `neither` in the arithmetic
// roster and are not loads, so no admission in this pass can ever name one.

// How far through the `leaving` batch the rebuild has read. It indexes the BATCH, never the block — the
// same split `SpliceBatchIndex` makes for the insert direction, and named apart from it because there is
// no splice here.
typealias LeavingIndex = int(0 to u64.max)

function keepingAllBut(refs OpIndexArray, leaving BlockOpPosArray) returns OpIndexArray
	var kept = OpIndexArray.create()
	var next = 0 as LeavingIndex

	for slot in 0 upto refs.count() 'eachRef'
		if next < leaving.count() and slot == (try leaving.get(next) otherwise panic("keepingAllBut: leaving.get OOB at {next}")) 'hoisted'
			next = next + 1
			continue
		end 'hoisted'

		kept.push(try refs.get(slot) otherwise panic("keepingAllBut: refs.get OOB at {slot}"))
	end 'eachRef'

	return kept
end 'keepingAllBut'

// Decide ONE op, recording its result as invariant when the answer is yes — the two phases' single
// admission point.
function admitsOp(module StdModule, opIdx OpIndex, mustExecute bool, memoryIsStable bool, scratch LicmScratch) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("admitsOp: op index {opIdx} from block.opRefs not in module.ops")

	classifyArithOperands(op, operands: scratch.arith)
	if scratch.arith.isArithmetic() 'phase1'
		return admitsPureComputation(op, scratch: scratch)
	end 'phase1'

	classifyLoadOperands(op, load: scratch.load)
	if scratch.load.isLoad() and memoryIsStable 'phase2'
		return admitsInvariantLoad(mustExecute, scratch: scratch)
	end 'phase2'

	return false
end 'admitsOp'

// Phase 1. A pure arithmetic op whose operands are all invariant, hoisted with no speculation question
// asked — `isPure` is the dialect's own licence to move it, and `requireArithOpIsPure` is what keeps the
// roster and the flag from drifting apart.
function admitsPureComputation(op StdOp, scratch LicmScratch) returns bool
	requireArithOpIsPure(op, reader: "hoistLoopInvariants")

	if not operandIsInvariant(scratch.arith.lhs, scratch: scratch) 'firstOperandVaries'
		return false
	end 'firstOperandVaries'

	if scratch.arith.hasSecondValueOperand() and not operandIsInvariant(scratch.arith.rhs, scratch: scratch) 'secondOperandVaries'
		return false
	end 'secondOperandVaries'

	scratch.invariant.insert(scratch.arith.resultId)
	return true
end 'admitsPureComputation'

// Phase 2. An invariant load, admitted under Rule 2a (its block must execute) or Rule 2b (a wider load
// through the same address is already being hoisted under 2a). Rule 1 has been checked by the caller.
//
// A load admitted under 2a is what makes 2b available to the loads after it, so the witness is recorded
// only on that path — a load riding on a witness proves nothing further about the object, because the
// span it proves is one the witness already proved.
function admitsInvariantLoad(mustExecute bool, scratch LicmScratch) returns bool
	if not operandIsInvariant(scratch.load.addrId, scratch: scratch) 'theAddressVaries'
		return false
	end 'theAddressVaries'

	if mustExecute 'ruleTwoA'
		scratch.witness.record(scratch.load.addrId, bytes: scratch.load.coverage())
		scratch.invariant.insert(scratch.load.resultId)
		return true
	end 'ruleTwoA'

	if not scratch.witness.coversAtLeast(scratch.load.addrId, need: scratch.load.coverage()) 'noWitness'
		return false
	end 'noWitness'

	scratch.invariant.insert(scratch.load.resultId)
	return true
end 'admitsInvariantLoad'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Applying the hoist
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Move the collected ops into the preheader, in the order they were collected.
//
// ⭐ APPENDED TO `opRefs`, which places them AFTER everything the preheader already computes and BEFORE
// its terminator — the only position that is right for both halves. An operand defined in the preheader
// itself is therefore already available, and the branch that enters the loop still comes last.
//
// The collection order is `analyzeLoopBlocks`' DOMINATOR-TREE PREORDER, so a hoisted op that feeds
// another hoisted op was collected first and lands first: an operand's defining block is an ancestor of
// its reader's, and a preorder visits an ancestor first. The def-before-use order the ops had inside the
// loop is the order they arrive in.
function appendToPreheader(module StdModule, func IrFunction, preheader BlockRefIndex, ops OpIndexArray)
	var block = blockAtPosition(module, func: func, position: preheader)

	for opIdx in ops 'eachHoisted'
		block.opRefs.push(opIdx)
	end 'eachHoisted'
end 'appendToPreheader'

// The block at one position in `func.blockRefs` — the descent every walk above makes, written once.
function blockAtPosition(module StdModule, func IrFunction, position BlockRefIndex) returns IrBlock
	let blockRef = try func.blockRefs.get(position) otherwise panic("hoistLoopInvariants: blockRefs.get OOB at {position}")
	return try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("hoistLoopInvariants: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
end 'blockAtPosition'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Scratch
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Rule 2a's answer for the CURRENT loop: how many of the loop's named blocks (its exits and its
// latches) each block lies on the dominator chain of, and how many chains were climbed. A block on all
// of them dominates all of them.
//
// ⚠ **DENSE OVER BLOCK POSITIONS, CLEARED BY THE POSITIONS IT TOUCHED — never by the block space.** A
// `growFilled(blockCount)` per loop is the Θ(loops × blocks) `LoopMembership`'s header measures, and it
// is the same defect one column over: this record is created once per module run and each loop pays only
// for the chains it actually climbed.
type MustExecuteCounter
	// Block position -> how many of the climbs below passed through it. A COUNT keyed by a position, so
	// it is a `DominatorChainCount` column and not a column of positions, whatever the two aliases have
	// in common underneath.
	var onChains as DominatorChainCountColumn
	// Every position `onChains` is non-zero at — what `reset` has to clear, and nothing more.
	var touched as BlockPositionColumn
	// How many chains were climbed for the current loop — one per exit and one per latch.
	var climbs as DominatorChainCount

	static function create() returns MustExecuteCounter
		return Self{onChains: DominatorChainCountColumn.create(), touched: BlockPositionColumn.create(), climbs: 0}
	end 'create'

	function reset(blockCount BlockRefIndex)
		for position in self.touched 'eachTouched'
			try self.onChains.set(position, value: 0) otherwise panic("MustExecuteCounter.reset: onChains.set OOB at {position}")
		end 'eachTouched'

		self.touched.clear()
		self.climbs = 0
		self.onChains.growFilled(blockCount as ElementIndex, value: 0)
	end 'reset'

	// Walk one block's immediate-dominator chain up to the loop header, counting every block on it. The
	// header terminates the walk because it dominates every member, so it is on the chain of each.
	function climbChainFrom(from BlockRefIndex, header BlockRefIndex, tree StdDominatorTree)
		self.climbs = self.climbs + 1

		var walk = from
		var moreToClimb = true

		while moreToClimb 'climb'
			let seen = try self.onChains.get(walk) otherwise panic("MustExecuteCounter.climbChainFrom: onChains.get OOB at {walk}")
			if seen == 0 'firstChainThroughIt'
				self.touched.push(walk)
			end 'firstChainThroughIt'
			try self.onChains.set(walk, value: seen + 1) otherwise panic("MustExecuteCounter.climbChainFrom: onChains.set OOB at {walk}")

			moreToClimb = walk != header
			if moreToClimb 'notTheHeaderYet'
				walk = tree.immediateDominatorOf(walk)
			end 'notTheHeaderYet'
		end 'climb'
	end 'climbChainFrom'

	// Whether `position` is on EVERY chain climbed — Rule 2a's test. A loop with no chains at all cannot
	// occur (a natural loop is found from a back edge, so it always has at least one latch), and the
	// equality would then be vacuously true for every block, which is why it is refused rather than
	// answered.
	function holds(position BlockRefIndex) returns bool
		if self.climbs == 0 'noChains'
			panic("MustExecuteCounter.holds: no dominator chain was climbed for this loop — a natural loop is discovered from a back edge and therefore always has a latch to climb from")
		end 'noChains'

		return (try self.onChains.get(position) otherwise panic("MustExecuteCounter.holds: onChains.get OOB at {position}")) == self.climbs
	end 'holds'
end 'MustExecuteCounter'

// How many of a loop's dominator chains pass through one block, and the column of those counts. Named
// apart from `BlockPositionColumn` for the reason `.claude/CLAUDE.md` gives — a typealias names a
// PURPOSE, not a representation, and these two happen to share one.
typealias DominatorChainCount = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias DominatorChainCountColumn = Array with DominatorChainCount

// Rule 2b's evidence: for each ADDRESS value, how far past it a load already hoisted under Rule 2a
// reads. TWO COLUMNS for one map, exactly as `ConstValueMap` and `CseExpressionTable` are — "is there an
// entry here" and "what it is" — because every `LoadCoverage` including 0 would be a legitimate value if
// one column had to carry both answers.
//
// A DENSE COLUMN keyed by `ValueId`, not a `Map`: a ValueId already IS a dense function-local index, so
// a hash set would spend a hash and a probe on something that is an array subscript
// (ARCHITECTURE.md §"Data representation"; `StdValueUseSet`'s header measures what breaking that costs).
// That is also what makes the witness test O(1) rather than a scan of the loads hoisted so far, which
// would have been quadratic in a loop that reads many fields of one record.
type LoadWitnessTable
	// WHICH addresses have a witness in the CURRENT loop — the presence half, and it IS `LoopValueStamps`
	// rather than a second copy of the stamp discipline. ⛔ Reviewed 2026-08-28 as this rung's one real
	// duplication: this record used to carry its own `stampOf`/`current` pair with a byte-identical
	// `beginLoop`, insert and staleness test, and the failure that invites is not a missed hoist — an edit
	// to the discipline in one copy and not the other lets a witness from a PREVIOUS loop read as present,
	// which is a load hoisted under Rule 2b on evidence that does not exist.
	var proven as LoopValueStamps
	// WHAT each of them proves. Two columns for one map, exactly as `ConstValueMap` is — "is there an
	// entry" and "what it is" — because every `LoadCoverage` including 0 is a legitimate value and a
	// single column would need one of them to mean "absent".
	var covers as LoadCoverageColumn

	static function create() returns LoadWitnessTable
		return Self{proven: LoopValueStamps.create(), covers: LoadCoverageColumn.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Forget every witness. A witness from one loop says nothing about another (its address value may not
	// even be invariant there), and the stamp makes that forgetting O(1).
	function beginLoop()
		self.proven.beginLoop()
	end 'beginLoop'

	// Record that a load through `addrId` reaching `proven` bytes is being hoisted unconditionally. The
	// WIDEST such load wins, because coverage is a lower bound on the object's size and the largest one
	// proven is the most that can be handed to Rule 2b.
	function record(addrId ValueId, bytes LoadCoverage)
		if bytes <= self.coverageOf(addrId) 'anotherLoadProvesMore'
			return
		end 'anotherLoadProvesMore'

		self.proven.insert(addrId)
		self.covers.growFilled(((addrId + 1) as ElementIndex), value: 0)
		try self.covers.set(addrId, value: bytes) otherwise panic("LoadWitnessTable.record: covers.set OOB at {addrId}")
	end 'record'

	// Whether an unconditionally-hoisted load through `addrId` already proves at least `need` bytes
	// readable — Rule 2b's test.
	function coversAtLeast(addrId ValueId, need LoadCoverage) returns bool
		return self.coverageOf(addrId) >= need
	end 'coversAtLeast'

	// How much THIS loop proves about `addrId`, with nothing proven answering 0 — which is sound in the
	// one direction it is read: `coversAtLeast` then answers false for every real load, because a load
	// transfers at least one byte.
	function coverageOf(addrId ValueId) returns LoadCoverage
		if not self.proven.contains(addrId) 'noWitnessInThisLoop'
			return 0
		end 'noWitnessInThisLoop'

		return try self.covers.get(addrId) otherwise panic("LoadWitnessTable.coverageOf: `proven` holds {addrId} but `covers` is only {self.covers.count()} long — the two columns are extended together by `record`")
	end 'coverageOf'
end 'LoadWitnessTable'

// ⭐⭐ **A DENSE PER-VALUE SET WHOSE RESET IS O(1), AND THE RESET IS THE WHOLE REASON IT IS NOT
// `StdValueUseSet`.** That type is the Std tier's one answer to "which values does this walk touch?" and
// it is right for its callers, every one of which builds a set once per FUNCTION: its `clear` keeps the
// column's capacity and the next `insert` re-extends it, which costs one pass over the value space per
// set built. This pass builds a set once per LOOP, and one pass over the function's value space per loop
// is Θ(loops × values) — the same shape `LoopMembership`'s header measures one column over, and MEASURED
// here as `phase:loopInvariantCodeMotion` still growing ×2.21 in bytes and ×2.42 in CPU per doubling
// after the block columns had been fixed.
//
// A GENERATION STAMP costs nothing to reset: entering a loop bumps one counter, and an entry stamped
// with an older generation reads as absent. `NoLoopStamp` is what the column grows filled with, and the
// first loop stamps 1, so an untouched slot can never match.
//
// THREE users, and they share this one implementation rather than three copies of it: the loop's defs,
// the subset of those proved invariant, and `LoadWitnessTable`'s presence half.
type LoopValueStamps
	var stampOf as LoopStampColumn
	var current as LoopStamp

	static function create() returns LoopValueStamps
		return Self{stampOf: LoopStampColumn.create(), current: NoLoopStamp}
	end 'create'

	function beginLoop()
		self.current = self.current + 1
	end 'beginLoop'

	function insert(valueId ValueId)
		self.stampOf.growFilled(((valueId + 1) as ElementIndex), value: NoLoopStamp)
		try self.stampOf.set(valueId, value: self.current) otherwise panic("LoopValueStamps.insert: stampOf.set OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'insert'

	function contains(valueId ValueId) returns bool
		if valueId >= self.stampOf.count() 'neverStamped'
			return false
		end 'neverStamped'

		return (try self.stampOf.get(valueId) otherwise panic("LoopValueStamps.contains: stampOf.get OOB at {valueId}")) == self.current
	end 'contains'
end 'LoopValueStamps'

// Which analysed loop an entry belongs to. `NoLoopStamp` is the value a freshly grown column holds and
// the one `beginLoop` counts up from, so it never collides with a real loop's stamp.
typealias LoopStamp = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias LoopStampColumn = Array with LoopStamp

let NoLoopStamp = 0 as LoopStamp

typealias LoadCoverageColumn = Array with LoadCoverage

// The mutable state one run of the pass carries. ONE object threaded through the walk rather than seven
// parameters at every level — they are created together, cleared together and reset together, and a walk
// that took them separately would have to name all seven at each of six call sites (`CseScratch`'s
// reason, and the same shape).
type LicmScratch
	// Every value the CURRENT loop defines, and the subset of those this pass has decided to hoist. Two
	// sets rather than one mutable "variant" set: a removal would need a second column anyway, and a
	// value moving from one set to the other is exactly what the analysis records.
	export var definedInLoop as LoopValueStamps
	export var invariant as LoopValueStamps
	// The loop currently being analysed, in both the forms the walks need — membership and a member list.
	export var members as LoopMembership
	// What that loop's ops do — Rules 1 and 3.
	export var effects as LoopEffects
	// Rule 2a's answer for that loop.
	export var mustExecute as MustExecuteCounter
	export var witness as LoadWitnessTable
	// The op indices to move, in the order they were collected.
	export var hoisted as OpIndexArray
	// The positions within the CURRENT block's `opRefs` that are leaving it — ascending, and reused per
	// block rather than allocated per block.
	export var leaving as BlockOpPosArray
	// The op being classified, in each of the two shapes it might have. Refilled per op and never
	// re-created — the shape `StdArithOperands` states.
	export var arith as StdArithOperands
	export var load as StdLoadOperands

	static function create() returns LicmScratch
		return Self{definedInLoop: LoopValueStamps.create(), invariant: LoopValueStamps.create(), members: LoopMembership.create(), effects: LoopEffects.create(), mustExecute: MustExecuteCounter.create(), witness: LoadWitnessTable.create(), hoisted: OpIndexArray.create(), leaving: BlockOpPosArray.create(), arith: StdArithOperands.create(), load: StdLoadOperands.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Empty every per-loop column, keeping capacity. ⛔ **A LEAKED ENTRY IS A MISCOMPILE, NOT A MISSED
	// HOIST** — the same shape `eliminateCommonSubexpressions` measured: a `ValueId` is FUNCTION-local, so
	// an `invariant` bit left over from the previous function names a value in a different value space,
	// and the op reading it would be hoisted out of a loop that computes it. Clearing per LOOP is
	// stricter than per function and makes both cases correct at one site.
	// ⚠ THREE columns are NOT reset here, each because its own owner resets it before its first read:
	// `members` (`collectNaturalLoop` clears it as it fills it), `effects` (`scanLoopEffects`) and
	// `leaving` (`analyzeLoopBlock`, per BLOCK rather than per loop). Clearing them twice would cost a
	// second walk and buy nothing. Every column that IS reset here is reset by the positions or ids the
	// last loop touched, never by the space it is dense over.
	function beginLoop(blockCount BlockRefIndex)
		self.definedInLoop.beginLoop()
		self.invariant.beginLoop()
		self.mustExecute.reset(blockCount)
		self.witness.beginLoop()
		self.hoisted.clear()
	end 'beginLoop'
end 'LicmScratch'
